  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Operator's Desktop",
    "overview": "The portfolio is a security engineer's workstation, seen late at night. The page is the desktop: a deep navy wallpaper drawn like a blueprint, with a faint 44px grid and two soft glows. Content lives in things you'd find on that desktop: OS windows with title bars and controls, cards set down on the grid, a shell prompt (`>_ cat cv.html`), and mouse pointers that move with intent. Every surface says \"this person works here\", without turning into a hacker cliché.\n\nThe system is **quiet at rest and alive on touch**. Surfaces are calm, dark, and low-contrast until the visitor does something: hovering lights an edge in Terminal Blue, the info window can be grabbed and dragged, a pointer double-clicks a section icon and the section opens like an app. Density is moderate. The content is proof (credentials, rankings, experience, live projects), so it must stay readable and fast to scan. Personality sits in the frames, cursors, and transitions, never on top of the evidence.\n\nMotion is part of the identity but is rationed. The intro, the section launches, and the occasional pointer burst are short, one-shot, and always disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion`. The design must never feel over-animated: if motion delays reading the proof, it is wrong.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark, atmospheric night palette on a blueprint grid; one blue accent for actions, one cyan accent for signals.",
      "OS-window framing: title bars, minimize/maximize/close controls, draggable windows.",
      "Terminal touches in monospace, used as accents, not as a theme.",
      "Custom gradient cursors (arrow and hand) shared by the real cursor and every animated pointer.",
      "Calm at rest, responsive on hover, drag, and click; rationed one-shot motion.",
      "Bilingual by construction: Geist for English, Thmanyah Sans for Arabic, logical (start/end) layout for RTL."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Lights Rule",
        "body": "Terminal Blue means \"you can act on this\"; Signal Cyan means \"this is live or notable\". Never swap them, and never introduce a third accent for UI chrome.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Blueprint Rule",
        "body": "Backgrounds are never flat black or pure gray. The wallpaper is always the navy gradient with the 44px grid and the two soft glows (blue top-left, cyan top-right).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pointer Palette Exception",
        "body": "The decorative visitor pointers may use their own gradient pairs (cyan/blue, pink/violet, amber/orange, emerald/teal, violet/indigo, rose). These colors belong to the pointers only and never leak into UI components.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shell Accent Rule",
        "body": "Monospace marks something as a command or a handle (`>_ cat cv.html`, `root`, `recruiter`). It is never used for body text, headings, or proof.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Equal Scripts Rule",
